  "evidence": "- TEXT_G: Balance the structural current budget by 2017/18; no rises in headline Income Tax/NI/VAT/Corporation Tax; invest at least £8bn more per year in the NHS by 2020; build 300,000 homes a year; expand free childcare to 20 hours (ages 2–4) and from nine months for working parents; stronger immigration controls with full entry/exit checks while welcoming skilled migrants/students; long‑term Industrial Strategy and apprenticeships; modern infrastructure and broadband.\n- TEXT_C: Replace austerity with modest spending increases; secure an extra £2bn for Scotland’s NHS; raise minimum wage to £8.70; scrap Bedroom Tax and reverse welfare changes; heavy emphasis on Scottish devolution/Home Rule and full fiscal responsibility; oppose Trident renewal; connect Scotland to HS2; 100,000 UK affordable homes per year; restore post‑study visas; oppose mass surveillance.",
  "reasoning": "UK‑wide voters prioritized the economy/deficit, the NHS, immigration, housing and family finances; TEXT_G addresses these head‑on with specific nationwide commitments on balancing the budget, NHS funding, immigration controls and a large housing build‑out alongside childcare and skills. TEXT_C covers some of these but is framed primarily around Scotland‑specific devolution and anti‑austerity aims, making it a narrower fit for the broad British electorate’s priorities.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "TEXT_G"
}
